{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>My blessing.</b> My gift — a gift that comes upon seeing someone's face, and for occasions, comes only for a greeting of peace. And every blessing that is for seeing a face — as in ״וַיְבָרֶךְ יַעֲקֹב אֶת פַּרְעֹה״ (Genesis 47:7), ״עֲשׂוּ אִתִּי בְּרָכָה״ of Sennacherib (II Kings 18), and likewise ״לִשְׁאָל לוֹ לְשָׁלוֹם וּלְבָרְכוֹ״ of Toi king of Hamath (II Samuel 8) — all of them are in the sense of a greeting of peace, which in לַעַז is called שלו״איר. So too here, בִּרְכָתִי is מוּ״ן שלו״ד — in the sense of a greeting of peace." "<b>That I have brought to you.</b> You did not toil in it, and I labored to bring it until it reached your hand (Bereshit Rabbah)." "<b>Gracious me.</b> The first nun has a dagesh, because it serves in place of two nuns — for he should have said חֲנָנַנְךָ, since there is no חָנֹן without two nuns. And the third [nun] is for [conjugational] use, like עָשַׂנִי, זְבָדַנִי." "<b>I have everything.</b> All my needs. And Esav spoke in the language of arrogance: יֶשׁ לִי רָב — more and more than I need."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "Rashi's final phrase מו״ן שלו״ד abbreviates מן שלום, 'of shalom / greeting'; the parallel French la'az שלו״איר = Old French saluer, 'to greet'.", 2 "Rashi explains the dagesh in the nun of חֻנַּנִי by analogy with forms like זְבָדַנִי (Genesis 30:20); he cites the hypothetical full form חֲנָנַנְךָ, which some witnesses read as חֲנָנַנִי — the grammar works either way."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:02:01.981167757Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}